 "intro": "Near Ruspina in Africa, January 46 BC. You are Julius Caesar, landed in Africa with too few troops. Your old legate Titus Labienus, now on Pompey’s side, comes against your foraging force with masses of Numidian horse and light infantry.",
 "debrief": "In the African War, written by one of Caesar’s officers, Caesar’s force was surrounded by Labienus’s Numidians, who shot into the packed recruits. Caesar extended his line and turned every second cohort to face the rear, then charged outward in both directions and broke the ring. As he fell back, Petreius attacked with fresh horse, and the Romans reached camp only at nightfall. Labienus had won the day, but Caesar’s army survived; he fortified Ruspina and waited for reinforcements, and won at Thapsus. The other choices are what-ifs.",
